window.P.push({"n":1,"pt":"I","ct":"What Medical AI Is, and What It Is Not","t":"Briefing Your Team on AI Categories","u":"Use this prompt when a clinical or administrative leader needs a shared, accurate internal vocabulary before evaluating any AI tool, so a rules-based alert, a generative chatbot, and a multi-step automated workflow stop being conflated under one loose label of \"AI\" in team conversations and vendor meetings.","no":"Claude is the strongest choice here because the deliverable is a disciplined, structurally consistent internal document, three fixed sections with headers, that a team will read and rely on afterward, and Claude's real strength is long-form structured output that faithfully holds a requested format across the full response. ChatGPT can produce a similar draft conversationally and is a reasonable second choice if the leader wants to iterate on tone through follow-up questions. Gemini adds little extra value for this particular prompt, since no large source document or image is involved.","v":["Act as an internal AI-literacy advisor helping a hospital department leader whose team currently uses \"AI\" as one loose catch-all term. Draft a one-page internal briefing for clinicians and administrators with no technical background that defines narrow AI, generative AI, and agentic AI in plain language, one short paragraph each, with one concrete example per category: an EHR-embedded predictive alert, a general-purpose assistant, and a multi-step automated workflow. Close with three questions the department should ask about any AI pitch to identify its true category. Use clear headers and no marketing language. Then ask me one or two clarifying questions about our department's actual tools so you can sharpen the examples, and tell me whether this briefing is a good candidate to save as a reusable Custom GPT for onboarding future leaders.","Act as an AI-literacy advisor for a hospital department that uses \"AI\" as one loose catch-all term. If I attach or paste any vendor one-pagers, pitch decks, or screenshots already circulating in our department, read them in full and pull real examples from that material rather than generic ones. Draft a one-page internal briefing for non-technical clinicians and administrators defining narrow AI, generative AI, and agentic AI in plain language, one paragraph each, each illustrated with one example: an EHR-embedded predictive alert, a general-purpose assistant, and a multi-step automated workflow. Close with three questions the department should ask about any new AI pitch to determine its true category. Use clear headers, stay concise, and avoid marketing language throughout.","You are advising a hospital department that currently uses 'AI' loosely and interchangeably, ahead of evaluating several AI tools. Draft a one-page internal briefing for clinicians and administrators with no technical background, structured in exactly three labeled sections. First, define narrow AI, generative AI, and agentic AI in plain language, one short paragraph each. Second, give one concrete example per category: an EHR-embedded predictive alert, a general-purpose assistant, and a multi-step automated workflow. Third, close with three questions the department should ask about any new AI pitch to determine which category it belongs to. Hold this exact structure precisely, use clear headers, keep every section concise, and avoid marketing language. If this briefing will anchor a Claude Project for ongoing vendor evaluations, write it as the first reference document to keep attached across future sessions."]});
window.P.push({"n":2,"pt":"I","ct":"The Modern Clinical AI Landscape","t":"Stress-Testing a Vendor's Written Claims","u":"Use this prompt during initial vendor screening, before formal due diligence begins, to separate a vendor's substantiated claims from unsupported marketing language in a product overview or whitepaper, so the evaluation team knows exactly what still needs independent verification.","no":"Gemini is the strongest choice because vendor whitepapers and product overviews often run long, sometimes twenty or more pages with embedded diagrams and screenshots, and Gemini's large context window and native multimodal handling let the full document be pasted or attached in one pass rather than pre-summarized, while also reasoning over any embedded charts or figures. ChatGPT is a reasonable second choice for a shorter, text-only one-pager. Claude is a strong option once the three lists need to be formatted into a polished internal memo for the governance committee described in Chapter 9.","v":["Below is the complete text of a vendor's product overview or whitepaper for a clinical AI tool: [paste the complete vendor document here]. Acting as a skeptical clinical AI evaluator, read the entire document and produce three separate lists: claims that cite a specific study, benchmark, regulatory clearance, or named health system; claims that use general marketing language, such as 'powered by AI' or 'clinically proven,' without a checkable source; and, for every item in the second list, a precise question our team should ask the vendor to substantiate it. Quote the exact claim language for every item. Then ask me one follow-up question about anything genuinely ambiguous before finalizing your lists, and don't soften or upgrade a vague claim as though it were already substantiated.","I am attaching or pasting the complete text of a vendor's product overview or whitepaper for a clinical AI tool, including any embedded charts, diagrams, or screenshots: [paste or attach the complete vendor document here]. Acting as a skeptical clinical AI evaluator, read the entire document in one pass, without pre-summarizing it, and produce three separate lists: claims that cite a specific study, benchmark, regulatory clearance, or named health system as evidence; claims that use general marketing language without citing any checkable source; and, for every item in the second list, a precise question our team should ask the vendor to substantiate it. Quote the exact claim language, including any claim embedded only in a figure or screenshot, for every item in all three lists, and do not soften or upgrade a vague claim as though it were already substantiated.","Below is the complete text of a vendor's product overview or whitepaper for a clinical AI tool: [paste the complete vendor document here]. Acting as a skeptical clinical AI evaluator, read the entire document and produce a structured memo with exactly three labeled sections: 'Substantiated Claims' (citing a specific study, benchmark, regulatory clearance, or named health system), 'Unsubstantiated Marketing Language' (general claims with no checkable source), and 'Verification Questions for the Vendor' (one precise question per item in the second section). Quote the exact claim language for every item, hold this three-section structure exactly, and do not soften, reinterpret, or upgrade any vague claim as though it were already substantiated. Write it so it can be dropped directly into the governance committee's evaluation file."]});
window.P.push({"n":3,"pt":"I","ct":"Data, Models, and the Clinical AI Pipeline","t":"Drafting Vendor Data-Provenance Questions","u":"Use this prompt before a vendor evaluation call for a new AI-based clinical tool, to build a structured, prioritized list of questions about training data, fine-tuning, retrieval grounding, and local validation expectations, refined interactively before the call takes place.","no":"ChatGPT is the strongest fit because this is an iterative, conversational drafting task where the assistant asks clarifying questions before producing a final structured list, a workflow instinct recent GPT-5.x updates specifically build on, and ChatGPT's broader ecosystem strength also suits turning this into a reusable, repeatable workflow later. Claude is a strong alternative once the final question list needs to be formatted into a formal vendor-facing document. Gemini adds little extra value here, since no long source document needs to be reasoned over in this particular prompt.","v":["Act as a clinical informatics colleague helping me prepare for a vendor evaluation call about a new AI-based clinical tool. First, ask me one question at a time to establish what category of tool this is, a narrow predictive model, a fine-tuned generative model, or a retrieval-augmented system, and what kind of data it will touch at our health system. Once I answer, generate a structured list of ten to fifteen questions I should ask the vendor, organized under clear subheadings covering training data provenance, whether our patient data would be used for further training, how the model was fine-tuned or grounded through retrieval, and what local validation the vendor recommends before go-live. Close by flagging the three most critical questions to resolve before any pilot begins. If this call recurs across vendors, tell me whether this is worth saving as a reusable Custom GPT for our team.","Act as a clinical informatics colleague helping me prepare for a vendor evaluation call about a new AI-based clinical tool. If I paste or attach the vendor's existing technical documentation or a prior submission in full, read it before asking anything. First, ask me one question at a time to establish what category of tool this is, a narrow predictive model, a fine-tuned generative model, or a retrieval-augmented system, and what kind of data it will touch at our health system. Once I answer, generate a structured list of ten to fifteen questions organized under clear subheadings covering training data provenance, patient-data reuse for further training, fine-tuning or retrieval grounding, and recommended local validation before go-live, grounded in anything the attached documentation already discloses. Close by flagging the three most critical questions to resolve before any pilot begins.","Act as a clinical informatics colleague helping me prepare for a vendor evaluation call about a new AI-based clinical tool. First, ask me one question at a time to establish what category of tool this is, a narrow predictive model, a fine-tuned generative model, or a retrieval-augmented system, and what kind of data it will touch at our health system. Once I answer, produce a structured, formally formatted document with clear subheadings covering training data provenance, patient-data reuse for further training, fine-tuning or retrieval grounding, and recommended local validation before go-live, containing ten to fifteen questions total. Close with a labeled 'Critical Before Pilot' section flagging the three most important items. Hold this structure precisely so the document can be sent directly to the vendor as written."]});
window.P.push({"n":4,"pt":"II","ct":"How Large Language Models Actually Work, in Practical Terms","t":"Confidence and Knowledge-Boundary Audit","u":"Use this prompt before relying on an AI assistant's answer to a clinical knowledge question, so the model explicitly separates what it is confident about from what may be outdated, uncertain, or unverifiable, rather than presenting everything in the same flat, confident tone.","no":"ChatGPT's recent GPT-5.x updates specifically targeted explaining uncertainty explicitly rather than asserting false confidence, and the model has been tuned against physician-authored rubrics for exactly this kind of calibrated, appropriately hedged health response, which is what this prompt asks it to demonstrate. Gemini is a reasonable alternative when the question requires reasoning over a large attached guideline document rather than the model's own recall. Claude also performs this well when the labeled three-part output needs to be pasted into a longer structured note afterward, since it holds formatting discipline reliably across a long answer.","v":["You are acting as a careful clinical reference assistant, not a diagnostic authority. Before you answer, state your approximate training data cutoff and whether you have real-time retrieval available in this conversation. Then answer my question in three clearly labeled sections: 'High confidence,' covering only well-established facts you are confident remain accurate; 'Time-sensitive or possibly outdated,' covering anything that changes with new guidelines, approvals, or evolving evidence; and 'Uncertain or requires verification,' covering anything you are inferring rather than recalling directly. Do not blend these categories together, and if any part of my question is ambiguous, ask me to clarify before answering. My question: [insert your specific clinical knowledge question here].","You are acting as a careful clinical reference assistant, not a diagnostic authority. I am pasting or attaching a full guideline document or chart excerpt below for you to reason from directly rather than from memory alone: [paste guideline or chart text here]. Before you answer, state your approximate training data cutoff and whether real-time retrieval is available in this conversation. Then answer my question in three clearly labeled sections: 'High confidence,' covering facts well-established or directly confirmed by the attached material; 'Time-sensitive or possibly outdated,' covering anything that changes with new guidelines or evidence; and 'Uncertain or requires verification,' covering anything inferred rather than directly stated in the attached text. Do not blend these categories. My question: [insert your specific clinical knowledge question here].","You are acting as a careful clinical reference assistant, not a diagnostic authority. Before you answer, state your approximate training data cutoff and whether you have real-time retrieval available in this conversation. Then answer my question in exactly three clearly labeled sections, held in this structure throughout: 'High Confidence,' covering only well-established facts you are confident remain accurate; 'Time-Sensitive or Possibly Outdated,' covering anything that changes with new guidelines, approvals, or evolving evidence; and 'Uncertain or Requires Verification,' covering anything you are inferring rather than recalling directly. Write this so it can be pasted, unedited, into a longer structured clinical note. Do not blend the three categories together. My question: [insert your specific clinical knowledge question here]."]});
window.P.push({"n":5,"pt":"II","ct":"Prompt Engineering Principles for Clinical and Administrative Use","t":"Structured Problem List Builder","u":"Use this prompt to convert a messy block of free-text clinical notes into a clean, consistently formatted, prioritized problem list, ready for a colleague to review or for inclusion in a structured handoff document.","no":"Claude is the strongest fit here because this task depends entirely on faithful adherence to a defined table structure and an explicit no-invention constraint held consistently across every row, exactly the kind of long, structurally disciplined output another clinician will rely on that Claude is built to sustain. ChatGPT is a reasonable alternative when the notes are short and the priority is a fast, conversational back-and-forth to refine the list interactively. Gemini becomes the better choice specifically when the raw notes are an entire multi-visit chart export too long to comfortably fit in a single exchange elsewhere.","v":["Act as a clinical documentation assistant helping a physician organize free-text notes into a structured problem list. I will paste raw clinical notes below. Using only information explicitly present in those notes, produce a table with exactly these columns: Problem, Status (active, resolved, or chronic-stable), Most Recent Relevant Finding, and Follow-Up Needed. Order rows from most to least clinically urgent. If a field cannot be completed from the notes, write 'not documented' rather than inferring a value, and do not add any problem, finding, or follow-up item not explicitly stated. After the table, list anything you were unable to place with confidence, then ask me one clarifying question if it would meaningfully change the table. Notes: [paste raw clinical notes here].","Act as a clinical documentation assistant helping a physician organize an entire chart export into a structured problem list. I am pasting or attaching the full multi-visit notes below, potentially spanning many encounters: [paste or attach the full chart export here]. Read all of it before responding, using only information explicitly present. Produce a table with exactly these columns: Problem, Status (active, resolved, or chronic-stable), Most Recent Relevant Finding, and Follow-Up Needed. Order rows from most to least clinically urgent, and for each row note which visit or date the finding came from so nothing gets lost across the long document. If a field cannot be completed, write 'not documented' rather than inferring a value. After the table, list anything you were unable to place with confidence.","Act as a clinical documentation assistant helping a physician organize free-text notes into a structured problem list. I will provide raw clinical notes below. Using only information explicitly present in those notes, produce a table with exactly these columns, held in this order without deviation: Problem, Status (active, resolved, or chronic-stable), Most Recent Relevant Finding, and Follow-Up Needed. Order the rows from most to least clinically urgent. If a field cannot be completed from the notes provided, write 'not documented' rather than inferring or inventing a value. Do not add any problem, finding, or follow-up item that is not explicitly stated in the notes. After the table, list any information you were unable to place with confidence. Notes: [paste raw clinical notes here]."]});
window.P.push({"n":6,"pt":"II","ct":"Choosing the Right Assistant: ChatGPT, Gemini, and Claude Compared","t":"Multimodal Long-Document Chart Review","u":"Use this prompt when a full discharge chart or multi-visit summary and an attached image, such as a wound photo, an ECG strip image, or a scanned imaging report, need to be reasoned about together in a single pass, without pre-summarizing either one first.","no":"Gemini is the strongest fit because this task needs both a long chart held in context without chunking and genuine multimodal reasoning across that text and an attached image in a single coherent pass, exactly the combination Gemini 3's native multimodal design and large vendor-described context window are built for. ChatGPT is a reasonable alternative when the image is simple and the priority is a fast, conversational follow-up discussion about the findings. Claude is the better choice instead when the deliverable needs to become a long, formally structured document, such as a formal consult note, rather than a working review summary.","v":["Act as a clinical documentation assistant helping a physician review a case that includes both a chart excerpt and an attached image. Using only information present in the chart text and visible in the attached image, produce three sections: 'Chart summary,' a concise timeline of clinically significant events; 'Image observations,' a plain, literal description of what is visible in the image, without offering a diagnosis; and 'Points of correlation,' noting only where the text and image appear to relate to the same finding, stated as observations rather than conclusions. Do not add any finding not explicitly present or visible. Then ask me one follow-up question if anything about the image is ambiguous. Chart text: [paste chart text here]. Image: [attach image here].","Act as a clinical documentation assistant helping a physician review a case that includes a long written chart excerpt and an attached image, such as a wound photo, an ECG strip image, or a scanned imaging report. Using only information present in the full chart text and visible in the attached image, produce three sections: 'Chart summary,' a concise timeline of clinically significant events across the entire text provided; 'Image observations,' a plain, literal description of what is visible in the attached image, without offering a diagnosis; and 'Points of correlation,' noting only where the chart text and image appear to relate to the same finding or timepoint, stated as observations rather than conclusions. Do not add any finding not explicitly present in the text or clearly visible in the image. Chart text: [paste full chart text here]. Image: [attach image here].","Act as a clinical documentation assistant helping a physician turn a case review into a formal consult note. Using only information present in the chart text and visible in the attached image below, produce a structured note with exactly three labeled sections: 'Chart Summary,' a concise timeline of clinically significant events; 'Image Observations,' a plain, literal description of what is visible in the image without a diagnosis; and 'Points of Correlation,' noting only where the text and image appear to relate to the same finding, stated as observations rather than conclusions. Hold this structure precisely and do not add any finding not explicitly present or visible. Chart text: [paste chart text here]. Image: [attach image here]."]});
window.P.push({"n":7,"pt":"III","ct":"FDA Pathways for AI/ML-Based Software as a Medical Device","t":"Vendor FDA Claim Verification Checklist","u":"Use this prompt when a governance committee member has a vendor's marketing materials and clearance letter or FDA database listing in hand and needs a structured, defensible comparison of what is actually claimed versus what is actually authorized, before the tool moves further through procurement.","no":"Claude is the strongest fit here because this is a long, structurally disciplined comparison memo intended to be read and relied on by a governance committee and possibly referenced in procurement documentation, exactly the kind of faithful, well-organized, conservative output Claude's institutional healthcare tooling and long-form writing discipline are built for. ChatGPT is a reasonable alternative for an initial quick pass or for drafting follow-up questions to send the vendor directly. Gemini becomes the better choice specifically when the intended-use documentation is a lengthy full FDA submission or database export that needs to be pasted in its entirety rather than excerpted.","v":["Act as a regulatory analyst supporting a hospital AI governance committee. I will provide a vendor's marketing description of an AI/ML clinical tool alongside the tool's actual FDA intended-use statement or database listing. Compare them and produce a memo with these sections: 'Marketing claim summary,' 'Actual FDA-authorized intended use,' 'Gaps or discrepancies, listed individually,' 'Regulatory pathway and any noted PCCP authorization,' and 'Recommended follow-up questions for the vendor's regulatory affairs contact.' Flag any claim that extends beyond the literal authorized intended use, even minor ones, and don't soften a discrepancy. After the memo, ask me one clarifying question about anything ambiguous before I bring this to committee. Marketing material: [paste text]. FDA statement or listing: [paste text].","Act as a regulatory analyst supporting a hospital AI governance committee. I am pasting or attaching, in full, both the vendor's marketing description of an AI/ML clinical tool and the complete FDA submission or database listing, including any lengthy technical sections: [paste or attach both documents in full here]. Read everything before responding. Compare them and produce a memo with these sections: 'Marketing claim summary,' 'Actual FDA-authorized intended use,' 'Gaps or discrepancies, listed individually with the page or section they came from,' 'Regulatory pathway and any noted PCCP authorization,' and 'Recommended follow-up questions for the vendor.' Flag any claim that extends beyond the literal authorized intended use, even minor ones, and do not soften a discrepancy to favor the vendor.","Act as a regulatory analyst supporting a hospital AI governance committee. I will provide a vendor's marketing description of an AI/ML clinical tool alongside the tool's actual FDA intended-use statement or database listing. Compare them systematically and produce a structured memo with exactly these sections, held in this order: 'Marketing Claim Summary,' 'Actual FDA-Authorized Intended Use,' 'Gaps or Discrepancies Between the Two, Listed Individually,' 'Regulatory Pathway and Any Noted PCCP Authorization,' and 'Recommended Follow-Up Questions for the Vendor's Regulatory Affairs Contact.' Be conservative: flag any claim that extends beyond the literal authorized intended use, even if the extension seems clinically minor, and never soften a discrepancy. Marketing material: [paste text]. FDA statement or listing: [paste text]."]});
window.P.push({"n":8,"pt":"III","ct":"The EU AI Act and International Regulatory Frameworks","t":"Cross-Jurisdictional Vendor Risk Questionnaire Draft","u":"Use this prompt when a procurement or compliance team needs a structured vendor risk questionnaire covering FDA, EU AI Act, and, where relevant, HTI-1 obligations for a clinical AI tool that may deploy across multiple jurisdictions, so the team is not relying on a single generic \"are you compliant\" question.","no":"Gemini is the strongest fit when the compliance team plans to paste an entire prior vendor submission, a full EU technical documentation package, or a lengthy FDA database export alongside this prompt for the model to reference directly while drafting, since its large context handling accommodates that material in one pass without pre-summarizing. Claude is the better choice once the questionnaire itself needs to become a long, formally structured document circulated for institutional sign-off. ChatGPT works well for a fast first draft or for iterating conversationally on individual question wording before the questionnaire is finalized.","v":["Act as a healthcare AI compliance analyst drafting a vendor risk questionnaire for a hospital procurement team. The tool is [describe the tool and its intended clinical use in two or three sentences]. It may deploy in the US and EU. Draft a questionnaire with sections for FDA status, EU AI Act status, and, if applicable, ONC HTI-1 disclosure availability. For each section, include three to five pointed questions a vendor's regulatory affairs contact should answer directly, and flag which answers should trigger legal review if unsatisfactory. Then ask me clarifying questions about anything underspecified in the tool description, and iterate on the specific question wording with me before I finalize it for procurement.","Act as a healthcare AI compliance analyst drafting a vendor risk questionnaire for a hospital procurement team. I am attaching a prior vendor submission, a full EU technical documentation package, or a lengthy FDA database export as reference material: [paste or attach the full source document here]. The tool in question is [describe the tool and its intended clinical use]. It may deploy in the US and EU. Using the attached material to ground and tailor the questions, draft a questionnaire with labeled sections for FDA status (intended-use, clearance pathway, PCCP boundaries), EU AI Act status (high-risk basis, CE marking, conformity documentation, human oversight design), and, if applicable, ONC HTI-1 disclosure availability. For each section, include three to five specific questions, and flag which unsatisfactory answers should trigger legal review.","Act as a healthcare AI compliance analyst drafting a vendor risk questionnaire for a hospital procurement team. The tool in question is [describe the tool and its intended clinical use in two or three sentences]. It may deploy in the United States and the European Union. Draft a structured questionnaire with exactly these labeled sections, held in this order: 'FDA Status' (intended-use statement, clearance pathway, PCCP boundaries), 'EU AI Act Status' (high-risk classification basis, CE marking, conformity assessment documentation, human oversight design), and, if applicable, 'ONC HTI-1 Disclosure Availability.' For each section include three to five specific questions a vendor's regulatory affairs contact should answer directly, and flag which unsatisfactory answers should trigger legal review. Write it as a document ready for institutional circulation and sign-off."]});
window.P.push({"n":9,"pt":"III","ct":"Institutional Governance, Model Cards, and Assurance Frameworks","t":"Draft a Governance Committee Model Card Summary","u":"Use this prompt when a governance committee has raw vendor documentation for an AI tool and needs it condensed into a standardized, model-card-style summary that highlights gaps and supports a clear go or no-go discussion, rather than requiring every committee member to read the full vendor packet independently.","no":"Claude is the strongest fit because this summary is a disciplined, faithfully structured document that a committee will read and act on directly, and Claude's institutional healthcare tooling and long-form structural reliability make it well suited to holding the exact section labels intact and clearly marking gaps rather than smoothing over missing information. Gemini is the better choice when the vendor documentation itself is an extensive technical package or a full regulatory submission that needs to be pasted in its entirety. ChatGPT works well for a fast conversational first pass or for drafting the committee's follow-up questions to the vendor once the summary has surfaced specific gaps.","v":["Act as an AI governance analyst preparing a model-card-style summary for a hospital committee reviewing a proposed clinical AI tool. Using the vendor documentation I provide, produce a summary with these sections: 'Intended use and validated scope,' 'Training and validation population characteristics,' 'Subgroup performance, or note explicitly if not disclosed,' 'Known limitations and inappropriate uses,' 'Regulatory status,' 'Update and retraining cadence,' and 'Documentation gaps requiring vendor follow-up.' Where the documentation doesn't address a section, write 'Not disclosed by vendor' rather than guessing. Keep it under 500 words. Then draft two or three follow-up questions I could send the vendor based on the gaps you found. Vendor documentation: [paste text].","Act as an AI governance analyst preparing a model-card-style summary for a hospital committee reviewing a proposed clinical AI tool. I am attaching the vendor's full technical package or regulatory submission, potentially lengthy: [paste or attach the complete vendor documentation here]. Read it in full before responding. Produce a structured summary with these sections: 'Intended use and validated scope,' 'Training and validation population characteristics,' 'Subgroup performance, or note explicitly if not disclosed,' 'Known limitations and inappropriate uses,' 'Regulatory status,' 'Update and retraining cadence,' and 'Documentation gaps requiring vendor follow-up.' Where the documentation does not address a section, write 'Not disclosed by vendor' rather than guessing or omitting the section. Keep the summary under 500 words.","Act as an AI governance analyst preparing a model-card-style summary for a hospital committee reviewing a proposed clinical AI tool. Using the vendor documentation I provide, produce a structured summary with exactly these section labels, held precisely in this order: 'Intended Use and Validated Scope,' 'Training and Validation Population Characteristics,' 'Subgroup Performance, or Note Explicitly if Not Disclosed,' 'Known Limitations and Explicitly Stated Inappropriate Uses,' 'Regulatory Status, FDA, EU AI Act, and HTI-1 as Applicable,' 'Update and Retraining Cadence,' and 'Documentation Gaps Requiring Vendor Follow-Up Before a Go or No-Go Decision.' Where documentation does not address a section, write 'Not disclosed by vendor' rather than omitting it or guessing. Keep the summary under 500 words. Vendor documentation: [paste text]."]});
window.P.push({"n":10,"pt":"IV","ct":"Ambient AI Scribes in Clinical Workflow","t":"Ambient Scribe Vendor Comparison Builder","u":"Build a structured, criterion-by-criterion comparison table across multiple ambient scribe vendor proposals or spec sheets before a purchasing decision, without losing track of which claim came from which document.","no":"Gemini is the strongest fit because the task depends on holding several full vendor documents in context at once rather than pre-summarizing them, and Gemini's large context window is a genuine, vendor-described differentiator for exactly this kind of multi-document, read-everything-before-you-answer task. ChatGPT can perform the same comparison reasonably well for two or three shorter proposals but starts losing document-to-claim traceability as the material grows. Claude is a better choice for the next step in this workflow, turning the finished comparison into a formal, structured procurement memo for a governance committee, once Gemini has produced the grounded comparison table.","v":["You are a clinical informatics analyst helping an outpatient practice select an ambient AI documentation scribe. I am pasting vendor materials for two or three products, spec sheets, security overviews, and pricing pages. Read all of it before responding. Build one comparison table with rows for EHR integration, supported specialties, real-time versus asynchronous note generation, data storage and retention, HIPAA and BAA availability, on-device versus cloud audio processing, coding suggestion capability, and pricing. For each vendor, note which section of the material supports each entry, and mark anything unaddressed as 'not specified, requires vendor follow-up.' Don't infer capabilities not explicitly stated, and ask me if you need any missing document.","You are a clinical informatics analyst helping an outpatient practice select an ambient AI documentation scribe. I am pasting or attaching vendor materials for multiple products below, in full, including spec sheets, security overviews, and pricing pages, potentially running to many pages per vendor: [paste or attach all vendor materials here]. Read all of the material in full before responding. Build one comparison table with rows for EHR integration and named platforms supported, supported specialties, real-time versus asynchronous note generation, data storage and retention policy, HIPAA and business associate agreement availability, on-device versus cloud audio processing, coding or E&M level suggestion capability, and pricing structure. For each vendor, note which specific section of the pasted material supports each entry, and mark any row the material does not address as 'not specified, requires vendor follow-up.' Do not infer capabilities not explicitly stated.","You are a clinical informatics analyst turning a finished ambient-scribe vendor comparison into a formal procurement memo. Using the comparison data I provide below, produce a structured document with these labeled sections, held precisely: 'Comparison Table' (EHR integration, supported specialties, real-time versus asynchronous note generation, data storage and retention, HIPAA and BAA availability, on-device versus cloud processing, coding suggestion capability, and pricing, with the source vendor document cited for each entry), 'Unresolved Items Requiring Vendor Follow-Up,' and 'Recommendation for Governance Committee Review.' Mark any unaddressed row as 'not specified, requires vendor follow-up' rather than inferring it, and do not soften an unresolved gap. Comparison data: [paste comparison data here]."]});
window.P.push({"n":11,"pt":"IV","ct":"Prompting for Structured Clinical Documentation","t":"Structured SOAP Note From Encounter Summary","u":"Turn a clinician's own shorthand or dictated summary of a patient encounter into a properly formatted SOAP note draft ready for review and entry into the record.","no":"Claude is best-fit because the entire safety model of this prompt depends on strict, disciplined adherence to a fixed four-section format and a conservative refusal to fill gaps with plausible but unstated content, both areas where Claude's structured long-form output and faithful instruction-following are a genuine, described strength. ChatGPT works acceptably for the same prompt and is a reasonable alternative if the clinician wants the model to ask a clarifying follow-up question about an ambiguous detail before finalizing. Gemini becomes the better choice only if the summary needs to be drafted alongside a long prior-note history or hospital course pasted in full for context.","v":["You are assisting a licensed clinician in drafting a SOAP-format progress note from their own summary of a patient encounter; you were not given any recording, transcript, or data beyond what appears below. Using only the information in my summary, produce a note with four labeled sections: Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan. Use the patient's own words where given, list only exam and vital sign findings I explicitly stated, list each problem with brief reasoning drawn from my summary, and list plan actions by problem including medication name, dose, and frequency exactly as given. Do not add any finding not present in my summary. Ask me one clarifying question about any section where my summary lacks enough detail before finalizing. My summary: [paste encounter summary here].","You are assisting a licensed clinician in drafting a SOAP-format progress note. I am pasting my summary of today's encounter alongside the patient's full prior-note history or hospital course for context: [paste today's summary, then paste the prior history or course in full]. Using only information explicitly stated in either document, produce a note with four labeled sections: Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan. Use the patient's own words where given, list only exam and vital sign findings explicitly stated, list each problem with brief reasoning informed by the prior history where relevant, and list plan actions by problem including medication name, dose, and frequency exactly as given. Do not add any finding not present in either document, and clearly flag any section lacking enough detail.","You are assisting a licensed clinician in drafting a SOAP-format progress note from their own summary of a patient encounter; you were not given any recording, transcript, or data beyond what appears below. Using only the information in my summary, produce a note with exactly four labeled sections, held in this order without deviation: Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan. In Subjective, use the patient's own words where I have given them. In Objective, list only exam and vital sign findings I explicitly stated. In Assessment, list each problem addressed with brief clinical reasoning drawn from my summary. In Plan, list actions by problem, including medication name, dose, and frequency exactly as given. Do not add any finding, diagnosis, or plan item not present in my summary, and clearly flag any section where my summary lacks enough detail. My summary: [paste encounter summary here]."]});
window.P.push({"n":12,"pt":"IV","ct":"Documentation Quality, Liability, and Human Review","t":"AI-Drafted Note Self-Audit Checklist","u":"Walk a clinician through a structured self-audit of an AI-assisted note draft before signing, catching unsupported content, redundant phrasing, and coding gaps in one pass.","no":"ChatGPT is best-fit here because the final step of the prompt, asking one genuine clarifying question before finalizing, plays directly to ChatGPT's conversational, follow-up-driven style, tuned specifically for exactly this kind of careful, one-more-question-before-you-commit health interaction. Claude is a strong alternative when the audit output itself needs to become a formal, structured quality-review document for a governance committee rather than a quick pre-signing check. Gemini becomes preferable only when the audit needs to compare the draft against a long prior chart history pasted in full, rather than a single encounter summary.","v":["Act as a documentation quality auditor. I will paste a clinical note draft produced with AI assistance, along with my own original brief summary of the encounter. Compare the two and produce a numbered audit list covering: any statement in the draft not traceable to my summary, flagged as unsupported; any language that looks copied forward from a prior visit; any element required for this visit type that appears missing; any redundant sentence or section that could be shortened; and any medication, dose, or coding suggestion needing independent verification. After the list, ask me one clarifying question about anything genuinely ambiguous before I finalize the note. My original summary: [paste]. AI-assisted draft: [paste].","Act as a documentation quality auditor. I am pasting a clinical note draft produced with AI assistance, my own original brief summary of today's encounter, and the patient's full prior chart history for comparison: [paste today's summary, the AI-assisted draft, and the full prior chart history]. Compare the draft against both my summary and the prior history and produce a numbered audit list covering: any statement in the draft not traceable to my summary; any language copied forward from a prior visit that doesn't match today's encounter, cross-checked against the actual prior notes; any element required for this visit type that appears missing; any redundant section that could be shortened; and any medication, dose, or coding suggestion needing independent verification.","Act as a documentation quality auditor producing a formal quality-review record. I will paste a clinical note draft produced with AI assistance, along with my own original brief summary of the encounter. Compare the two and produce a structured document with exactly these labeled sections: 'Unsupported Statements' (not traceable to my summary), 'Possible Carried-Forward Language' (does not match today's encounter), 'Missing Required Elements' for this visit type, 'Redundant Content' that could be shortened without losing meaning, and 'Items Requiring Independent Verification' (medications, doses, coding). Hold this structure precisely so it can be filed as a documentation quality record. My original summary: [paste]. AI-assisted draft: [paste]."]});
window.P.push({"n":13,"pt":"V","ct":"Radiology and Imaging AI in Practice","t":"Radiology AI Vendor Evaluation Brief","u":"Use this prompt when a radiology or imaging leadership team needs a structured, board-ready evaluation document comparing a candidate imaging AI vendor's claims against the questions a department should resolve before signing a purchase agreement.","no":"Claude's strength in long, structurally disciplined written output that another clinician or committee member will rely on makes it the right choice for a procurement document with a strict section order and a policy-compliant, hedged tone throughout. ChatGPT is a reasonable second choice if the team wants a more conversational back-and-forth while refining the brief section by section. Gemini becomes the better choice instead if the vendor material itself is long, a full technical white paper or a lengthy validation study PDF pasted in whole, since its large-context handling avoids the need to pre-summarize that source material before the evaluation can begin.","v":["Act as a clinical AI procurement analyst supporting a hospital radiology department. I will give you a vendor's product description and marketing claims for an imaging AI tool. Produce a structured evaluation brief with these sections in order: a one-paragraph plain-language summary of what the tool does, classified as triage, detection, or quantification; a list of specific questions to ask the vendor about FDA clearance scope, validation population, and scanner compatibility; a table of workflow integration considerations for PACS and worklist systems; and a short list of local monitoring metrics. Use conservative, hedged language and flag any unsupported claim. After the brief, ask me any clarifying question that would help refine a specific section further. Vendor material: [paste vendor product description here].","Act as a clinical AI procurement analyst supporting a hospital radiology department. I am attaching the vendor's full technical white paper or validation study, potentially running to many pages: [paste or attach the complete vendor document here]. Read it in full before responding. Produce a structured evaluation brief with these sections in order: (1) a one-paragraph plain-language summary of what the tool actually does, classified as triage, detection, or quantification; (2) specific questions about FDA clearance scope, validation population, and scanner compatibility, citing the page or section of the attached document that answers or fails to answer each; (3) a table of workflow integration considerations for PACS and worklist systems; (4) local monitoring metrics appropriate to this tool's function. Use conservative, hedged language and flag any claim not supported by the attached material.","Act as a clinical AI procurement analyst supporting a hospital radiology department. I will give you a vendor's product description and marketing claims for an imaging AI tool. Produce a structured evaluation brief with exactly these sections in this order, held precisely: (1) a one-paragraph plain-language summary of what the tool actually does, classified as triage, detection, or quantification; (2) a list of specific questions the department should ask the vendor about FDA clearance scope, validation population, and scanner compatibility; (3) a table of workflow integration considerations for PACS and worklist systems; (4) a short list of local monitoring metrics appropriate to this tool's function. Use conservative, hedged language throughout and flag any claim not supported by a cited study or clearance detail. Vendor material follows: [paste vendor product description here]."]});
window.P.push({"n":14,"pt":"V","ct":"Pathology and Laboratory AI","t":"Pathology AI QA Gap Analysis","u":"Use this prompt when a laboratory director or pathology quality officer needs to compare a long source document, a published validation study, a vendor technical white paper, or a CAP accreditation checklist, against the laboratory's own current QA protocol text, to identify specific gaps before or after deploying a digital pathology AI tool.","no":"Gemini's ability to hold a large source document, or two, in context in full without pre-summarization is the reason it is best-fit here: a validation study and an internal QA protocol pasted in whole can run to many pages, and asking the model to reason across both documents in a single pass avoids the information loss that chunking or manual pre-summarization would introduce. Claude is a strong second choice if the output document itself needs heavier structural discipline for a formal accreditation submission. ChatGPT is a reasonable choice for a faster, more conversational first pass when the two source documents are shorter.","v":["Act as a laboratory quality assurance advisor. I am pasting two documents: a published validation study or vendor technical description for a digital pathology AI tool, and our laboratory's current internal QA protocol document. Compare them and produce: a table listing every scanner, stain protocol, and reagent detail from the validation study alongside whether our protocol matches, differs, or is unspecified; a list of concordance-tracking elements present in the validation study but missing from our protocol; and a prioritized list of re-validation triggers to add. Use hedged, conservative language and don't assert equivalence unless the text explicitly supports it. Then ask me one clarifying question before finalizing the gap list. Documents: [paste both documents].","Act as a laboratory quality assurance advisor. I am pasting or attaching, in full, two documents: a published validation study or vendor technical description for a digital pathology AI tool, and our laboratory's current internal QA protocol document, both of which may run to many pages: [paste or attach both documents in full here]. Read both completely before responding. Compare them and produce: (1) a table listing every scanner, stain protocol, and reagent detail specified in the validation study alongside whether our protocol matches, differs, or is unspecified; (2) a list of concordance-tracking elements present in the validation study but missing from our QA protocol; (3) a prioritized list of re-validation triggers we should add, referencing specific gaps identified. Use hedged, conservative language and do not assert equivalence unless the pasted text explicitly supports it.","Act as a laboratory quality assurance advisor preparing material for a formal accreditation submission. I am pasting two documents: a published validation study or vendor technical description for a digital pathology AI tool, and our laboratory's current internal QA protocol document. Compare them and produce a structured document with exactly these labeled sections, held precisely: 'Scanner, Stain, and Reagent Concordance Table' (matches, differs, or unspecified against our protocol), 'Concordance-Tracking Elements Missing From Our QA Protocol,' and 'Prioritized Re-Validation Triggers to Add,' each item referencing the specific gap it addresses. Use hedged, conservative language throughout, and do not assert equivalence between our setup and the validation study's setup unless the pasted text explicitly supports it. Documents follow: [paste validation study text, then paste our QA protocol text]."]});
window.P.push({"n":15,"pt":"V","ct":"Prompting Multimodal AI for Image-Assisted Reasoning","t":"Plain-Language Imaging Report Explainer","u":"Use this prompt after a radiologist or ordering clinician has already signed and finalized an imaging report, to draft a plain-language explanation a patient can read before or during a follow-up visit. This prompt works from the report's text, not from the underlying image, and its output must be reviewed by the ordering clinician before it reaches a patient.","no":"ChatGPT is best-fit here because of its documented strength in patient-facing plain-language explanation and its model behavior tuned toward flagging uncertainty and encouraging appropriate follow-up rather than asserting false confidence, both directly relevant to a patient-education task. Claude is a strong alternative when the health system needs the output to follow a strict institutional template or reading-level policy with disciplined, auditable structure. Gemini becomes preferable instead when the source material includes a long multi-report history that needs to be synthesized in full alongside the most recent report, rather than a single report in isolation.","v":["Act as a patient educator helping a clinician prepare a plain-language explanation of an already-finalized, signed radiology report. I will paste the report's impression and findings text below, already interpreted and signed by a radiologist. Do not add any new interpretation, diagnosis, or clinical judgment beyond what is stated. Rewrite the findings and impression in plain language at an eighth-grade reading level, define any medical terms you keep, note explicitly that the ordering clinician will explain what the results mean for the patient's care, and end with two or three example questions the patient might want to ask at their follow-up visit. Then ask me if the intended reading level or tone needs adjusting for this patient. Report text follows: [paste finalized, signed report text here].","Act as a patient educator helping a clinician prepare a plain-language explanation of an imaging finding. I am pasting the patient's most recent finalized, signed radiology report along with the full prior imaging report history for this patient, so the explanation reflects the trend over time: [paste the most recent signed report, then the prior report history in full]. Do not add any new interpretation, diagnosis, or clinical judgment beyond what is stated in the reports. Rewrite the most recent findings and impression in plain language at an eighth-grade reading level, briefly note how they compare to the prior reports using only stated comparisons, define any medical terms you keep, note that the ordering clinician will explain what the results mean for the patient's care, and end with two or three example follow-up questions.","Act as a patient educator helping a clinician prepare a plain-language explanation of an already-finalized, signed radiology report, formatted to our institution's patient-communication template. I will paste the report's impression and findings text below. Do not add any new interpretation, diagnosis, or clinical judgment beyond what is stated. Produce output with exactly these labeled sections, held in this order: 'Plain-Language Findings' (eighth-grade reading level, medical terms defined), 'What This Means for Your Care' (a neutral statement that the ordering clinician will explain implications), and 'Questions to Ask at Your Visit' (two or three examples). Hold this structure precisely for auditability. Report text follows: [paste finalized, signed report text here]."]});
window.P.push({"n":16,"pt":"VI","ct":"General-Purpose LLMs as Point-of-Care Reference Tools","t":"Point-of-Care Evidence Check with Confidence Flags","u":"Use this prompt when you need a fast answer to a clinical question from a general-purpose assistant and want the response structured so its evidentiary weaknesses are visible rather than hidden behind confident phrasing, before you decide what, if anything, still needs independent verification.","no":"Use ChatGPT because recent GPT-5.x model updates were specifically tuned for health-response quality, including explaining uncertainty rather than asserting false confidence and recognizing when a question needs escalation, behavior this prompt is designed to draw out explicitly. Claude is a close alternative when the answer needs to be pasted into a longer structured document for a colleague to review. Gemini becomes the better choice the moment the underlying question requires reasoning over a full guideline PDF or chart excerpt pasted directly into context rather than a standalone question.","v":["You are functioning as a point-of-care clinical reference assistant for a licensed clinician who will independently verify anything before acting on it. I will describe a specific clinical question. Answer using only well-established, widely accepted medical knowledge, and explicitly flag any part of your answer involving a rapidly evolving guideline, a rare interaction, or a numeric dosing value. For each major claim, state your confidence level as high, moderate, or low, and name the type of source that would confirm it. End with a short list of exactly what I should independently verify. If my question is ambiguous or missing key clinical detail, ask me before answering. My question: [insert clinical question here].","You are functioning as a point-of-care clinical reference assistant for a licensed clinician who will independently verify anything before acting on it. I am pasting or attaching a full guideline PDF or chart excerpt directly relevant to my question, so you can reason from the actual source rather than recall alone: [paste or attach the full guideline or chart excerpt here]. I will also describe a specific clinical question. Answer using the attached material plus well-established medical knowledge, and explicitly flag any part of your answer involving a rapidly evolving guideline, a rare interaction, or a numeric dosing value not clearly stated in the attached text. For each major claim, state your confidence as high, moderate, or low and cite whether it came from the attachment or general knowledge. End with exactly what I should independently verify. My question: [insert clinical question here].","You are functioning as a point-of-care clinical reference assistant for a licensed clinician who will independently verify anything before acting on it and will paste this answer into a longer structured note for a colleague. I will describe a specific clinical question. Answer using only well-established, widely accepted medical knowledge, in a structure held precisely: 'Answer' (using explicit confidence flags of high, moderate, or low for each major claim), 'Flagged for Rapid Evolution or Numeric Precision' (any guideline, interaction, or dosing value needing extra care), and 'Verify Before Use' (exactly what to check, and against what source type, such as a current package insert or specialty guideline). My question: [insert clinical question here]."]});
window.P.push({"n":17,"pt":"VI","ct":"Prompting for Differential Diagnosis and Clinical Reasoning Support","t":"Structured Differential Diagnosis Reasoning Prompt","u":"Use this prompt when a clinician wants an AI assistant to generate a structured, auditable differential diagnosis with explicit supporting and opposing evidence for each candidate, to widen or stress-test an independently formed clinical impression before finalizing a workup plan.","no":"Use Claude because this prompt demands faithful adherence to five distinct structural sections across up to six candidate diagnoses without the response drifting into looser, less auditable prose, exactly the long-form structural discipline Claude is built around. ChatGPT is a reasonable alternative during the earlier, iterative phase of refining an incomplete case description, since it tends to surface clarifying questions before committing to an answer. Gemini becomes preferable when the case history is long enough that it needs to be pasted in full, an entire chart export or multi-visit record, rather than pre-summarized before the prompt is run.","v":["Act as a clinical reasoning support tool for a licensed clinician who will make the final diagnostic decision. Case: [insert chief complaint, relevant history, exam findings, relevant negatives, and available labs or imaging here]. If the case as described feels incomplete, ask me one or two clarifying questions first. Then generate a structured differential diagnosis: up to six candidate diagnoses ranked by clinical likelihood given only what I provided; for each, one sentence of supporting evidence and one sentence against; any immediately dangerous diagnosis that must be excluded first even if less likely; the specific additional finding or test that would most change this ranking; and an explicit statement of your overall confidence and what limits it. Do not present this as a diagnosis or a recommendation to act on.","Act as a clinical reasoning support tool for a licensed clinician who will make the final diagnostic decision. I am pasting the patient's full chart export or multi-visit record below rather than a summarized case, so relevant history isn't lost to pre-summarization: [paste the full chart export or multi-visit record here]. Using only what appears in the attached record, generate a structured differential diagnosis with these exact sections: up to six candidate diagnoses ranked by clinical likelihood; for each, one sentence of supporting evidence and one sentence against, citing where in the record it came from; any immediately dangerous diagnosis that must be excluded first; the specific additional history, exam finding, or test that would most change this ranking; and an explicit statement of your overall confidence and what limits it. Do not present this as a diagnosis or a recommendation to act on.","Act as a clinical reasoning support tool for a licensed clinician who will make the final diagnostic decision. Case: [insert chief complaint, relevant history, exam findings, relevant negatives, and available labs or imaging here]. Generate a structured differential diagnosis with exactly these five sections, held precisely in this order and this format across every candidate: first, up to six candidate diagnoses ranked by clinical likelihood given only what I provided; second, for each, one sentence of supporting evidence and one sentence of evidence against; third, any immediately dangerous diagnosis that must be excluded first even if less likely overall; fourth, the specific additional history, exam finding, or test that would most change this ranking; fifth, an explicit statement of your overall confidence and what limits it. Do not present this as a diagnosis or a recommendation to act on."]});
window.P.push({"n":18,"pt":"VI","ct":"Guardrails: Hallucination, Overconfidence, and Verification Habits","t":"Self-Critique and Uncertainty Audit Prompt","u":"Use this prompt to audit an AI-generated clinical statement you are about to rely on, whether from an earlier session or from a colleague, before it enters a note, an order, or a clinical discussion, by having an assistant actively hunt for unverified specifics rather than defend the original text.","no":"Use ChatGPT because recent health-response tuning specifically favors explaining uncertainty rather than defending an existing answer, which suits this prompt's adversarial, error-hunting framing. Claude is an equally strong choice when the flagged text is long or will feed into a structured document, such as an appeal letter or a formal case writeup, because its output discipline keeps the audit itself organized and traceable. Reach for Gemini instead when the claim under review is embedded inside a much larger source document, a full note or chart export, that needs to be pasted in alongside it for the audit to make sense in context.","v":["I am about to rely on the following AI-generated clinical statement in a note or discussion: [paste the exact AI output or claim here]. Acting as a skeptical reviewer, not as the original author, identify every specific factual claim, numeric value, drug name, dose, citation, or guideline reference in this text. For each one, state whether it is well-established, stable knowledge or should be independently checked against a primary source before use, and name the type of source I should check. Do not simply restate or defend the original text; actively look for errors, unsupported specifics, and citations that sound plausible but may not be real. If anything is genuinely unclear, ask me for more context before finishing your review.","I am about to rely on an AI-generated clinical statement embedded inside a larger document. I am pasting the full note or chart excerpt below, with the specific statement under review marked clearly: [paste the full source document here, with the statement to review marked]. Acting as a skeptical reviewer of the marked statement, using the surrounding document only for context, identify every specific factual claim, numeric value, drug name, dose, citation, or guideline reference in the marked text. For each, state whether it is well-established knowledge or needs independent verification against a primary source, naming the source type. Do not simply restate or defend the original text; actively look for errors, unsupported specifics, and plausible-sounding but potentially fabricated citations.","I am about to rely on the following AI-generated clinical statement, which will feed into a longer structured document such as an appeal letter or case writeup: [paste the exact AI output or claim here]. Acting as a skeptical reviewer, not as the original author, produce a structured audit with exactly these sections: 'Claims Requiring No Further Check' (well-established, stable knowledge), 'Claims Requiring Independent Verification' (with the specific source type to check for each), and 'Suspected Errors or Fabricated-Sounding Citations.' Hold this structure precisely so the audit itself stays organized and traceable. Do not simply restate or defend the original text; actively hunt for errors and unsupported specifics."]});
